# =============================================================================
# Docker Ignore File for Node.js Tutorial Backend Application
# =============================================================================
# This .dockerignore file specifies files and directories to exclude from the
# Docker build context when building the Node.js tutorial backend container.
# It ensures minimal, secure, and efficient container builds by excluding
# unnecessary, sensitive, or development-only files from being sent to the
# Docker daemon during the image build process.
#
# Educational Purpose:
# - Demonstrates Docker best practices for Node.js applications
# - Shows security considerations for containerized applications
# - Illustrates build optimization techniques for faster, smaller images
# - Provides clear documentation for each exclusion pattern
#
# Technical Requirements Addressed:
# - Minimal Build and Distribution Requirements (Section 8.2)
# - Security and Best Practices (Section 3.2.3, 8.2.2)
# - Educational Value and Code Clarity (Section 1.2.3, 8.2.3)
#
# Build Context: Project root directory (matches docker-compose.yml context: ./)
# Target Dockerfile: infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NODE.JS AND NPM DEPENDENCIES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Exclude node_modules directory (always rebuilt in container)
# - node_modules contains platform-specific binaries and compiled dependencies
# - Dependencies are installed fresh in container via 'npm ci' for consistency
# - Prevents host/container OS and architecture mismatches
# - Significantly reduces build context size (can be hundreds of MB)
# - Ensures reproducible builds with exact versions from package-lock.json
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# NPM and Yarn debug/error logs
# - Contains debugging information not needed in production images
# - May include sensitive information about system configuration
# - Generated during local development and troubleshooting
# - Prevents accidental inclusion of error traces in container images
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
lerna-debug.log*
.npm/
.yarn/

# Package manager lock files for non-npm managers
# - Only package-lock.json is needed for npm ci
# - Yarn and pnpm lock files are unnecessary for npm-based builds
# - Prevents confusion about which package manager to use
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml

# NPM cache and temporary files
# - npm cache should not be included in container images
# - Temporary files created during package installation
# - .npmrc may contain sensitive registry configurations
.npmrc
.yarnrc*
.pnpm-store/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUILD OUTPUT AND TEMPORARY FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Build artifacts and distribution directories
# - dist/ and build/ contain compiled/processed application code
# - These directories are created by build processes like webpack, tsc, etc.
# - Not needed in container as source code is used directly
# - For production builds, only final artifacts should be included
dist/
build/
out/
lib/
tmp/
temp/

# TypeScript compilation output
# - .tsc_cache contains TypeScript compiler cache
# - Not needed in runtime container environment
# - Source TypeScript files are not used in this project
.tsc_cache/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Webpack and bundler outputs
# - Generated by frontend build tools
# - Not applicable to this backend-only application
# - Included for completeness and future extensibility
webpack-assets.json
webpack-stats.json
.webpack/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TESTING AND COVERAGE FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Test directories and files
# - test/ and tests/ contain unit and integration tests
# - Test files are not needed in production container images
# - Reduces image size and attack surface
# - Prevents accidental exposure of test data or credentials
test/
tests/
__tests__/
spec/
specs/

# Test file patterns
# - Various test file naming conventions used in JavaScript projects
# - .test.js, .spec.js, and similar patterns
# - Covers both JavaScript and TypeScript test files
# - Ensures comprehensive exclusion of test-related files
*.test.js
*.test.ts
*.test.mjs
*.spec.js
*.spec.ts
*.spec.mjs
**/test/**
**/tests/**
**/__tests__/**

# Test coverage reports
# - coverage/ directory contains test coverage analysis
# - Generated by Jest, NYC, or other coverage tools
# - Contains HTML reports and coverage data
# - Not needed in production containers
coverage/
.coverage/
.nyc_output/
lcov.info
*.lcov

# Jest configuration and cache
# - Jest testing framework cache and configuration
# - .jest/ contains cached test data for faster subsequent runs
# - Not needed in production environment
.jest/
jest.config.*

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENVIRONMENT AND CONFIGURATION FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Environment variable files
# - .env files may contain sensitive configuration data
# - Should be injected at runtime via container environment variables
# - Prevents hardcoding secrets into container images
# - Follows security best practices for containerized applications
# - .env.example is excluded to prevent confusion in production
.env
.env.*
.environment

# Local development configuration
# - Configuration files specific to local development
# - May contain developer-specific settings or credentials
# - Should not be included in container images
.envrc
.env.local
.env.development
.env.test
.env.production

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOGGING AND DEBUG FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Application and system logs
# - Log files should not be included in container images
# - Containers should log to stdout/stderr for orchestration
# - Prevents sensitive information leakage through logs
# - Reduces container image size
*.log
logs/
log/
.logs/

# Debug files and crash dumps
# - Created during application crashes or debugging sessions
# - May contain sensitive runtime information
# - Not needed in production container images
# - Platform-specific debug information
*.pid
*.seed
*.pid.lock
pids/
core.*

# Node.js inspector files
# - Created when using Node.js debugger or inspector
# - Contains debugging session data
# - Security risk if included in container images
.vscode-inspect/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERSION CONTROL AND DEVELOPMENT TOOLS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Git version control
# - .git/ directory contains entire repository history
# - Significantly increases build context size
# - May contain sensitive information in commit history
# - .github/ contains CI/CD workflows not needed in containers
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.gitlab-ci/

# SVN version control (legacy)
# - Subversion version control directories
# - Included for completeness
.svn/

# Mercurial version control
# - Mercurial version control directories
# - Included for completeness
.hg/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPERATING SYSTEM FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# macOS system files
# - .DS_Store contains macOS finder metadata
# - Created automatically by macOS Finder
# - Not needed in Linux containers
# - Common source of build context bloat
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db

# Windows system files
# - Windows-specific metadata and thumbnail files
# - Not needed in Linux containers
# - Prevents Windows-specific artifacts in cross-platform builds
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Linux/Unix system files
# - Various Unix system files and directories
# - Temporary and cache directories
.directory
.fuse_hidden*
.nfs*

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IDE AND EDITOR FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Visual Studio Code
# - VSCode workspace settings and extensions
# - Developer-specific configuration
# - Not needed in container environment
.vscode/
*.code-workspace

# JetBrains IDEs (WebStorm, IntelliJ IDEA)
# - IDE configuration and cache files
# - Developer-specific settings and plugins
# - Can be quite large and not needed in containers
.idea/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws

# Sublime Text
# - Sublime Text project and workspace files
# - Developer-specific configuration
*.sublime-project
*.sublime-workspace

# Vim/Neovim
# - Vim editor swap and backup files
# - Created during editing sessions
# - Not needed in production containers
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.netrwhist

# Emacs
# - Emacs editor backup and autosave files
# - Developer-specific configuration
*~
\#*\#
/.emacs.desktop
/.emacs.desktop.lock
*.elc
auto-save-list/
tramp
.\#*

# Atom
# - Atom editor configuration
.atom/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DOCUMENTATION AND README FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Documentation files
# - README files are not needed in runtime containers
# - Documentation should be maintained separately from container images
# - Reduces image size while maintaining security
# - API documentation is available externally
*.md
*.MD
*.markdown
*.rst
*.txt
README*
CHANGELOG*
CONTRIBUTING*
LICENSE*
NOTICE*

# Documentation directories
# - docs/ contains comprehensive API and usage documentation
# - Not needed in production container runtime
# - Available through external documentation systems
docs/
doc/
documentation/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROJECT MANAGEMENT AND CI/CD FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Package manager configuration
# - .yarnrc, .npmrc may contain registry credentials
# - Package manager specific configuration files
# - Not needed in container runtime environment
.yarnrc
.yarnrc.yml
.pnpmfile.cjs

# CI/CD configuration files
# - Continuous integration and deployment configurations
# - Platform-specific workflow definitions
# - Not needed in runtime containers
.travis.yml
.circleci/
.jenkins/
Jenkinsfile
.gitlab-ci.yml
.buildkite/
appveyor.yml
azure-pipelines.yml

# GitHub Actions workflows
# - Already excluded via .github/ but included for clarity
# - Contains CI/CD pipeline definitions
.github/workflows/
.github/actions/

# Docker-related files (excluding current .dockerignore)
# - Other Docker configurations not needed in build context
# - docker-compose files for development orchestration
# - Multiple Dockerfile variants for different purposes
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
docker-compose*.yaml
.dockerignore
!infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile
!infrastructure/docker/.dockerignore

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY AND CERTIFICATE FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SSL/TLS certificates and private keys
# - Certificate files should not be baked into container images
# - Private keys must never be included in containers
# - Should be mounted as secrets or environment variables at runtime
*.crt
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.der
ssl/
certs/
certificates/

# SSH keys and configuration
# - SSH private keys are highly sensitive
# - Should never be included in container images
# - Could allow unauthorized access if compromised
.ssh/
id_rsa*
id_dsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*

# GPG keys
# - Cryptographic keys for signing and encryption
# - Should not be included in container images
*.gpg
*.asc
.gnupg/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKUP AND TEMPORARY FILES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Backup files
# - Various backup file patterns from editors and systems
# - Temporary files created during development
# - Not needed in production containers
*.bak
*.backup
*.old
*.orig
*.save
*.tmp

# Archive files
# - Compressed archives often used for backups or distribution
# - May contain sensitive data or outdated code
# - Should not be included in container images
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.tar.bz2
*.tbz2
*.tar.xz
*.txz
*.rar
*.7z

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PLATFORM-SPECIFIC EXCLUSIONS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Node.js platform-specific
# - Node.js REPL history contains executed commands
# - May include sensitive data or credentials
# - npm and yarn cache directories
.node_repl_history
.npm/
.yarn/
.pnp.*

# Runtime directories
# - Directories created at runtime
# - Should be created by container, not included in image
runtime/
run/
var/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEVELOPMENT UTILITIES AND TOOLS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Linting and formatting configuration
# - ESLint, Prettier, and other tool configurations
# - May be useful to include for development containers
# - Excluded for minimal production images
.eslintcache
.stylelintcache
.sass-cache/

# Monitoring and profiling
# - Performance monitoring and profiling output
# - Generated during development and testing
# - Not needed in production containers
.clinic/
clinic.json
*.heapsnapshot
*.cpuprofile

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KUBERNETES AND ORCHESTRATION
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Kubernetes configuration files
# - Deployment manifests and configurations
# - Not needed within container images
# - Managed separately by orchestration systems
k8s/
kubernetes/
*.k8s.yaml
*.k8s.yml
kustomization.yaml
kustomization.yml

# Helm charts
# - Kubernetes package manager configurations
# - Not needed in container runtime
helm/
charts/
Chart.yaml
values.yaml

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD PLATFORM SPECIFIC
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# AWS specific files
# - AWS configuration and credentials
# - Should never be included in container images
# - Use IAM roles or environment variables instead
.aws/
aws-config.json

# Google Cloud Platform
# - GCP service account keys and configuration
# - Highly sensitive and should not be in containers
.gcloud/
*.json

# Azure specific
# - Azure configuration files
# - Should use managed identities instead
.azure/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EDUCATIONAL NOTES SECTION
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The following patterns demonstrate comprehensive Docker best practices:
#
# 1. SECURITY PRINCIPLES:
#    - Never include credentials, keys, or certificates in images
#    - Exclude configuration files that may contain sensitive data
#    - Prevent information disclosure through logs and debug files
#
# 2. BUILD OPTIMIZATION:
#    - Exclude large directories like node_modules (rebuilt in container)
#    - Remove documentation and development tools to reduce image size
#    - Prevent unnecessary files from being sent to Docker daemon
#
# 3. CONSISTENCY AND REPRODUCIBILITY:
#    - Exclude platform-specific files (.DS_Store, Thumbs.db)
#    - Remove IDE and editor configurations
#    - Ensure builds are identical across different development environments
#
# 4. EDUCATIONAL VALUE:
#    - Each exclusion is documented with clear rationale
#    - Demonstrates industry-standard containerization practices
#    - Shows security considerations for production deployments
#
# 5. NODE.JS SPECIFIC CONSIDERATIONS:
#    - node_modules exclusion is critical for consistent builds
#    - Test files and coverage reports are development-only
#    - Environment files should be injected at runtime for security
#
# This .dockerignore file serves as both a functional build optimization tool
# and an educational resource demonstrating Docker best practices for Node.js
# applications. It ensures that only necessary files are included in the
# container build context, resulting in smaller, more secure, and faster builds.